<p><strong>Cortisol+ shows your available sleep data in the same view as your wearable stress and recovery signals.</strong></p>
<p>It helps you look back at your nights and routines.</p>
<p>It does not measure next-morning cortisol, find the cause of a wake-up, or tell which sleep stage changed your hormones.</p>

<h2>What sleep data comes from Apple Watch?</h2>
<p><strong>Apple Health can show your estimated time asleep</strong>, plus periods marked as awake, REM, core and deep sleep.</p>
<p>What you see depends on your watch, software, settings and how long you wear it.</p>
<p>Apple's <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108906">sleep tracking guide</a> explains setup and how to look at your sleep history.</p>
<p>These are wearable estimates, not a clinical sleep study.</p>

<h2>How does sleep count in Cortisol+?</h2>
<p><strong>Sleep has a 20% base weight in the wellness score.</strong> That's before adjustments for missing data and other parts of the model.</p>
<p>The app reads the sleep records you allow and turns them into one factor in the score.</p>
<p>That 20% is a choice in how the software is designed. It does not mean sleep makes up 20% of your cortisol production. Read the full <a href="/how-it-works/">scoring methodology and limitations</a>.</p>
<p>A change in the overall score can come from several signals, how recent they are, and the model's assumptions.</p>
<p>Comparing two nights can't single out the effect of alcohol, a supplement or one sleep stage. We don't promise that a personal hormone pattern will show up after two or four weeks.</p>

<ZenTip image="/app-ui/zen_new_tour_sleep.webp">
  Charge your watch <strong>before dinner</strong>, not at bedtime, so it has battery left to track the whole night.
</ZenTip>

<h2>How do I get started with sleep tracking?</h2>
<ol>
  <li>Set up sleep tracking by following Apple's instructions. Wear a charged, comfortably fitted watch to bed.</li>
  <li>Check that the night shows up in Health before you look for it in Cortisol+.</li>
  <li>Review the app's Health permissions and choose which available records to share.</li>
  <li>Note the date of the sleep record and the times of other readings. They may cover different periods.</li>
</ol>
<p><strong>If sleep or HRV is missing, start with the source data and permissions.</strong></p>
<p>Our <a href="/blog/apple-watch-hrv-not-updating/">missing-data troubleshooting guide</a> walks through those checks for heart rate variability (HRV).</p>
<p>Turning on read access doesn't create a reading the watch never recorded.</p>

<CreatorShort videoId="2tlBIlKwf6M" title="A daily sleep and stress tracking routine" creator="Jizil" duration="PT56S" summary="Jizil shows sleep-stage views, wearable-derived insights and Zen’s guided breathing. Compare the app’s available sleep records with your own routine and observations; the video does not establish that a sleep change caused a hormone change." />

<h2>Keep an observation journal</h2>
<p><strong>Use the <a href="/resources/stress-sleep-journal/">seven-day stress and sleep journal</a></strong> to write down bedtime, wake time, routines and how you felt.</p>
<p>Then compare your notes with the records in the app.</p>
<p>You can print it or download a blank CSV. The page does not send your entries to analytics.</p>

